#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/id #uuid "1c2e1a0e-4e7b-5029-94c3-3eb14d8869bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-222" :question/problem "A cube is shrinking uniformly at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm}^3/\\text{s}.$ How fast is each side of the cube shrinking when the volume of the cube is $27\\,\\text{mm}^3?$\n\n![](images/0c/0ca7021995b1a501f6cb862b33649bdb1927f7ce4a8630a9e261382a193f40a4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V=27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV=x^3\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V = 27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 27 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm dt}=-3$ into the expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -3 & =3(3)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are shrinking at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{9}\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=3\\,\\textrm{mm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/id #uuid "74034d81-c90c-5ab4-b27c-aa1d7aca70cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-223" :question/problem "All of the edges of a cube are increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the surface area of the cube changing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,\\text{cm}^3?$\n\n![](images/a6/a6ef2af1ed8e44f82469625e232ed67c0b809a3c8199a6ce52fd2bf2e3fcffa7.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d S}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS= 6x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}S & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 8 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2\\,\\text{cm}=20\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe can now plug the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12(20)(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =480.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of  $480 \\,\\text{mm}^2/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/id #uuid "6694f63e-e83a-5597-81cb-80af3c132524" :question/math-academy-id "e-224" :question/problem "Water is being poured into a rectangular prism-shaped tank. The length and width of this tank are $20 \\textrm{ ft}$ and $15 \\textrm{ ft},$ respectively. If the height of the water increases at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{ft/min}$, determine the rate at which the volume of stored water increases.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7c6749ec0585ab714640fa9155eba212e2a9c628b50cc3c3de07492b8b3aec.png)" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=20\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  W=15\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of the water in the tank:\n\n$$\nV = LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(20)(15)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =300\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is rising at a rate of $50\\,\\text{ft}^3\\textrm{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab47776-0aac-5751-a91c-c1bc7c35d2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522176} {:answer/id #uuid "790db2f0-5257-5b8a-86e2-065177a863ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522257} {:answer/id #uuid "8237770d-ccc6-56ab-a4b5-c7878730f2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522279} {:answer/id #uuid "a04f8d85-00af-5c6c-b617-1c2abd0264df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522350} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8fbffd-c5b1-58f2-af4a-dc00b4611c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8237770d-ccc6-56ab-a4b5-c7878730f2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522279} :answer-field/id #uuid "09c4b278-6409-5796-a889-b0dd450f73c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e7d3cb4-0661-5b5b-a138-b4024c524f85" :question/math-academy-id "q-116066" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2be88ec9cb7559ca8d80d528e143d4f2dd72109c9dc576b353dd352855c53ff.png)\n\nA cube is expanding uniformly at a rate of $135{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is each edge of the cube increasing when the volume of the cube is $125{\\text{ cm}}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=135\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=125\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=125\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 125 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=5$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=135$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 135 & =3(5)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are increasing at a rate of $\\frac{9}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=5\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4331b15a-72a2-5d98-9fc3-899e4d9a04fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522166} {:answer/id #uuid "9832c67a-2d5f-5d3a-8360-50943f6ba6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522331} {:answer/id #uuid "dd65f9e7-be1f-50f3-829c-d1434e73e451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522481} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c3fe14-8f54-52bd-bfdb-9a39b3520805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522518} {:answer/id #uuid "f48115a9-c89d-51fa-b46a-7491efcdf077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f48115a9-c89d-51fa-b46a-7491efcdf077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522538} :answer-field/id #uuid "94705966-f953-5894-bfa7-9571835bf295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3878760-f553-50af-9094-65f6b89b14da" :question/math-academy-id "q-116266" :question/problem "![](images/00/0060643c716e8f9c6d0420c419ba1d6efd7c3dc986279feb4ceae9f860dfb313.png)\n\nThe length of the rectangular prism shown above is increasing, while the $4\\,\\text{in}$ width and the $6\\,\\text{in}$ height don't change. What is the rate of change of the length of the rectangular prism if the volume is increasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ z & =6\\,\\text{in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=4\\,\\text{in}$ and $z=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=x(4)(6)=24x.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(24x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =24(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =24(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the rectangular prism is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ce561d-4554-50ea-b082-6ff5e35c97e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522106} {:answer/id #uuid "6c74f04b-bcfa-5467-9414-e11ffa53e63f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522239} {:answer/id #uuid "90ecd540-4787-5529-b79c-562d35ce6e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522315} {:answer/id #uuid "ed69a781-7c92-5ec0-b5f9-cb723c492b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522526} {:answer/id #uuid "eedee9f0-a7c7-5bdf-8d5b-a60ae7bd02f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed69a781-7c92-5ec0-b5f9-cb723c492b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522526} :answer-field/id #uuid "8155f6ea-7ad1-5d0c-9428-1db9bd0cca29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c0a4827-45c4-5e42-a7d2-ab4337115042" :question/math-academy-id "q-116486" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f87cf02aac4cee2680b2045b13fc2e7ce006c39c37fa470a7803c56624d6acd.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the $6\\,\\text{cm}$ height does not change. How fast is the surface area of the rectangular prism decreasing when its dimensions are $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $11\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =11\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =6\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=6\\,\\text{cm}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=12x+2xy+12y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12x+2xy+12y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+12)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(12+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 4+12)(5)+(12+2\\cdot 11)(-4) \\\\  & =100-136 \\\\  & =-36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "896f763d-06f2-5ad2-a422-baec40f25127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522296} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2fd06e-ebdc-5efb-9f47-fcfd77c60ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522302} {:answer/id #uuid "ce363e11-7033-5f84-a491-d1ab09cce555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522444} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b90a68-307d-55af-925d-ba90f1e97c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522453} {:answer/id #uuid "e0490112-c171-5687-8b4a-c7699a51cb6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98.15\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "896f763d-06f2-5ad2-a422-baec40f25127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522296} :answer-field/id #uuid "f82c44d4-53de-55bc-86e0-85f055a9f683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f3094c1-9493-5020-b906-a8b3df41f18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-30020" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f84ba9d0dbbf8f10fc3244c5ca3a79d4d619ab2360ac5b77fe88ddc0943dd68f.png)\n\nA rectangular swimming pool (with a horizontal bottom) is being drained. If its length and width are $25\\text{ m}$ and $15\\text{ m}$, respectively, and the water level is falling at a rate of $0.25\\,\\text{m/min},$ how fast is the water draining out of the pool?" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the swimming pool, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=25\\,\\text{m},\\,W=15\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(25)(15)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =375\\cdot (-0.25) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-93.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water is draining out at a rate of $93.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bcf9157-1f36-52e9-a61e-f8e687fb3456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522118} {:answer/id #uuid "4be5baa3-e1d0-576d-8ab9-af6042eedd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.25\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522182} {:answer/id #uuid "9dde3e4d-67fd-59dc-b0bb-4064cd68c048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522343} {:answer/id #uuid "a51d42a0-8eab-5b68-be60-237b93b5dc6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522368} {:answer/id #uuid "fce26c00-7a81-52cc-9e1d-4d762eae2284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4be5baa3-e1d0-576d-8ab9-af6042eedd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.25\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522182} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24d6138-77a7-51ac-903f-75f16a69daee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a0ddd70-eefe-5886-baf8-3017327746b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-30027" :question/problem "![](images/03/03e31c64b26e304e720ed0178ff23af8b2ca0955c678bd695e930e0a5cb9069a.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $0.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}$ and its height increasing at a rate of $1\\text{ feet/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $10{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $2\\text{ feet}$ in length, $3\\text{ feet}$ in width and $1\\text{ feet}$ in height?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\text{ feet}, \\\\ y & =1\\text{ feet}, \\\\ z & =3\\text{ feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-10{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=1\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(2)(1)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(2)(3)(1)+(1)(3)(-0.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-7.25\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $7.25\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=1\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1b4802-4fbf-597a-8717-edd9da5ff601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500498} {:answer/id #uuid "1d66b81f-aab7-5dee-bc76-93149d565137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500528} {:answer/id #uuid "651e5094-ed0f-5287-afa0-88760a288cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44\\,100{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500678} {:answer/id #uuid "70ef183c-3e32-589d-bdef-3a17eb486140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,150{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500700} {:answer/id #uuid "c387d300-dcb3-5156-be2d-bccac09b58bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d66b81f-aab7-5dee-bc76-93149d565137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500528} :answer-field/id #uuid "7049e9d7-8f84-594a-912b-f5fcab69cbfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d8efcf9-a827-5e33-8982-8c950b1390fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-30081" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea7c818a6694ba959c5230dcde8611a7b1dc44bcad383f54c93d2934336c35b6.png)\n\nThe edges of a cube decrease at a rate of $9\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the volume decreasing when the length of each edge is $40\\text{ cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=40\\text{ cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=40$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3(40)^{2}\\cdot (-9)\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-43\\,200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}$ at the moment when $x=40\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad11d165-ba21-5fa4-bbce-11ab5622f378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "624" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad11d165-ba21-5fa4-bbce-11ab5622f378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "624" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522380} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6a5f66-96e9-5ca5-964a-29cec265540a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84586114-96d4-5e09-89f7-bf9841d73d0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-314521" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8c4a2802cbd06159b0b0dcbee59f5b4a6e93b4c4e029dc88d04352c50471659e.png)\n\nAn elastic rectangular prism whose height is $15\\text{ in}$ and width is $24\\text{ in}$ is being stretched. If its length is increasing at a rate of $8\\text{ in/min},$ what is the rate of change of the surface area of the rectangular prism?\n\nThe surface area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. The variables $x,S,$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =15\\text{ in}, \\\\ z & =24\\text{ in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nS=2(x)(24)+2x(15)+2(15)(24)=78x+720.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =78(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the above expression using $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=8,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =78(8)=624.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rectangular prism's surface area is increasing at a rate of $624\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed7400b8-da75-5dd3-ba2a-9ffd605e0895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed7400b8-da75-5dd3-ba2a-9ffd605e0895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522527} :answer-field/id #uuid "67580179-a44d-55e9-9d4c-66e88565b431" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dec6d3d-90b6-5bbb-9401-3e793424773e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343177" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dde167688f1693d34f07f89c923843643769369f767fec68e3578e910d8da3fb.png)\n\nA sugar cube is dissolving uniformly in water. If the volume of the cube decreases at a rate of $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},$ determine the rate at which the edges of the cube are decreasing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ Assume that the edge's rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe edges of the cube are decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 8 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=2$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-48$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -48 & =3(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -48 & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges of the cube are decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c4bd551-965b-5c88-811a-93ad01e05d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c4bd551-965b-5c88-811a-93ad01e05d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522184} :answer-field/id #uuid "89f2717d-a35e-5e3f-aba4-917081b91c68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "deaa15dc-da8f-5e37-b1fc-ea24a0a7f029" :question/math-academy-id "q-343191" :question/problem "![](images/69/69b4b3c2488cf394df687d7446ab5a49bab037de936402f02d43196f71dddf09.png)\n\nTwo glass plates are held a constant distance apart, forming a growing sheet of laminated glass resin. As rollers pull the plates apart, the length is increasing by $2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ while the $1\\,\\text{cm}$ thickness does not change. If resin is injected so that the volume of the sheet is increasing at a rate of $32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?\n\nThe width is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =1\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=1\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $z$ is not changing and $z=1,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(xy) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+y(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+4(2) \\\\ 32 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+8 \\\\ 24 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2.4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=1\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5a0c29-6e4d-5a80-af9f-64a6b2caa904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f5a0c29-6e4d-5a80-af9f-64a6b2caa904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522189} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad2e690-1a98-50c7-805e-a491fbfad8c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59e7f49b-ab56-5848-a273-6624c60395ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-343210" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0bea5354a71b12e82cba561901db0fc97de873eb6f9985f0deb15afba5da801.png)\n\nA cube-shaped sugar cube is dissolving uniformly in tea. If the surface area of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ at what rate are the edges changing when each edge is $2\\,\\text{cm}?$ Assume that each edge is decreasing at the same rate, and the rate of decrease of the edge is positive.\n\nThe cube's edge is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -24 & =12(2)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are decreasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89474594-05e8-543d-b7ea-9e4daa46e36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89474594-05e8-543d-b7ea-9e4daa46e36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522295} :answer-field/id #uuid "12874409-02a6-5ec7-bc52-1cd515da4b29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13a313ba-f3f4-5888-a2ed-231c29e8a4fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343310" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4723361163d4ca372019a91a347d2cb3d0aeee0ece1ace62762348dede9cea8.png)\n\nA worker is coating a surface with epoxy using a thickness gauge, which fixes the epoxy height at $5\\,\\text{in}.$ If the length is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}$ and the width is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},$ how fast is the surface area of the epoxy layer increasing when its length is $12\\,\\text{in}$ and its width is $7\\,\\text{in}?$\n\nThe surface area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =12\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ y & =7\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=5\\,\\text{in}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=10x+2xy+10y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10x+2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(10+2x)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(10+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(10+2\\cdot 12)(2)+(10+2\\cdot 7)(3) \\\\  & =(34)(2)+(24)(3) \\\\  & =68+72 \\\\  & =140\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is increasing at a rate of $140\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "637d44e3-fc00-59b5-9b83-bb2c15f53289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "637d44e3-fc00-59b5-9b83-bb2c15f53289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522225} :answer-field/id #uuid "996f61b9-bb6c-58a3-b6ed-c482bde482da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e145a618-7b18-50b4-9616-c05db1f4edaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343322" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e700536730a443e47e56bf33cd7dcb460032b72984d5aaaaf75d1de987c6b495.png)\n\nA factory uses a rectangular mold with a fixed width of $18\\,\\text{in}$ and a fixed height of $10\\,\\text{in}.$ Liquid resin is poured in so that the resin fills the mold and the length increases at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}$ as the front face moves. What is the rate of change of the resin's volume in the mold?\n\nThe resin's volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $H$ and $W$ be the height and width of the rectangular mold, respectively, and\n\n- let $L$ and $V$ be the length and volume of the resin, respectively.\n\nNote that $L$ and $V$ change with time, while $H$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nH=10\\,\\text{in},\\,W=18\\,\\text{in},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $L,$ is given by the formula for the volume of resin:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $H$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =WH\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(18)(10)\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =180\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =540.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resin's volume is increasing at a rate of $540\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d285a60-7326-5698-83d2-37558960d4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d285a60-7326-5698-83d2-37558960d4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522264} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3e231d-940f-56e2-b89e-9c44df37d8f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ad65545-bf82-508f-8a9d-4bdd8ed0f623" :question/math-academy-id "q-343323" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b0876986e02941b588bf9bacbc5a0caa61eb2b95021fa4ed52408dd4f5d3f72.png)\n\nA rectangular block of clay is being reshaped as it moves through a press. Its height is decreasing at a rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/min},$ its width is increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{ ft/min},$ and its length is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{ft}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{ft}$ in width, and $3\\,\\text{ft}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of volume is positive.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=10\\text{ ft},\\, & W=4\\text{ ft}, \\\\ H=3\\text{ ft},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ ft/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=1\\text{ ft/min},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5\\text{ ft/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(4)(3)(-2)+(10)(3)(1)+(10)(4)(-0.5) \\\\  & =-14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of $14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7bc959-ffc0-515f-8e2d-91ce2f38383f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f7bc959-ffc0-515f-8e2d-91ce2f38383f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522348} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b46e6b-bbfb-526b-9cc5-0bb7578fda4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13eab2a4-15b8-5aae-888d-a5c1961fc009" :question/math-academy-id "q-343334" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac1e157b59bb632068e45776cdeb6bdc2dee42fd7f9b92ee2ea61401c1087d29.png)\n\nA rectangular block of ice is melting in a narrow channel that keeps its length fixed at $12\\,\\text{in}.$ Its width is decreasing by $1\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h},$ while its height is decreasing by $0.4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h}.$ How fast is its volume decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{in}$ in width and $4\\,\\text{in}$ in height? Assume that the volume's rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the ice block, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the ice block.\n\nNote that $H,$ $W,$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=12\\text{ in},\\, & W=5\\text{ in}, \\\\ H=4\\text{ in},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ in/h}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\text{ in/h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $W$ and $H,$ is given by the formula for the volume of ice block:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $L$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(WH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L[W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(12)(5)(-0.4)+(12)(4)(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-24+(-48) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of ice is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f79d57-7057-5d04-9cfc-2b04e2a421f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522097} {:answer/id #uuid "2c36ed08-005a-59b5-8732-844b9503b009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522119} {:answer/id #uuid "381c9c71-7212-5a03-8354-5571704318c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522140} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3a5042-2b9f-58d0-aa25-271eb4f26711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.5{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522149} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8d429c-3e5b-567b-a273-b93fb30b35c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.5{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c36ed08-005a-59b5-8732-844b9503b009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\text{ feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522119} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a6724f-08c0-556a-8d01-69c8d214a05a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bacb6b56-6056-5c36-b7bd-9714bddbfa56" :question/math-academy-id "q-49011" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f50ef86e6663531757afb15dac45705de30ccfaae4d7079bc5a9cf474b7467f7.png)\n\nIn a laboratory, a researcher is increasing the edges of an iron cube at a rate of $0.5\\text{ feet/min}.$ How fast is the surface area changing when each edge is $3\\text{ feet?}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.5\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=3\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12(3)(0.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of $18\\,{\\text{feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"}]}
